<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="color-scheme" content="dark"><title>Sentiment Radar — 2026-09-28</title><style id="dashboard-dark-theme">:root{color-scheme:dark}*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0;background:#0b1320;color:#e7effb;font:15px/1.65 'Segoe UI',Arial,sans-serif}main{max-width:1200px;margin:auto;padding:30px 24px}a{color:#83b4ff}h1{font-size:34px;line-height:1.15}h2{margin-top:36px;font-size:24px}h3{margin:6px 0 12px;font-size:22px}p{margin:10px 0}.muted,.sources{color:#a9b9cc}.sources{font-size:12px}.eyebrow{color:#8bdac8;text-transform:uppercase;font-size:11px;letter-spacing:1px}.grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:16px}article,.market{padding:22px;background:#142033;border:1px solid #304258;border-radius:14px}article{margin:12px 0}.metrics{padding:12px 14px;background:#0b1729;border-left:3px solid #7e9dff;font-size:13px}details{padding:14px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #304258}summary{cursor:pointer}blockquote{color:#b5c7dc;border-left:3px solid #37557a;margin:12px 0;padding-left:16px}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%}td,th{padding:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #304258;text-align:right}td:first-child,th:first-child{text-align:left}.scroll{overflow:auto}footer{margin:32px 0;color:#9bafc8;font-size:13px}@media(max-width:750px){.grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}main{padding:22px 14px}h1{font-size:28px}}</style></head><body><nav aria-label="Wiki navigation" style="background:#101c2e;color:#a6b7ce;border-bottom:1px solid #2d4059;padding:10px 24px;font:14px Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif"><a href="../index.html" style="color:#79adff;text-decoration:none">← Wiki dashboards</a><span style="margin:0 12px">/</span><a href="index.html" style="color:#79adff;text-decoration:none">Sentiment Radar</a><span style="margin:0 12px">/</span><a href="http://DS-CAP-33:8080/dashboards/#sentiment-radar" style="color:#79adff;text-decoration:none">Main dashboards</a><span style="margin:0 12px">/</span><a href="daily-brief.html" style="color:#79adff;text-decoration:none">Daily briefing</a><span style="margin:0 12px">/</span><a href="score-returns.html" style="color:#79adff;text-decoration:none">Score → returns</a></nav><main><p><a href="index.html">All dashboards</a> · <a href="http://DS-CAP-33:8080/dashboards/#sentiment-radar">Main hub</a></p><div class="eyebrow">Daily review · 2026-09-28</div><h1>AI interest broadens; optics gains substance, but buying conviction remains uneven</h1><p>LITE has a more specific specialist-sales case despite a small X audience. NVDA has fresh buyback and agent-security announcements; META remains the broadest discussion among today’s highlighted names. The caution list separates memory skepticism and execution risk from simple cooling in attention. JPM reports interested investors still reluctant to add exposure, while Barclays describes different HF and long-only flows. Neither establishes market-wide crowded holdings. Comparison is with the last verified email delivery on 24 September; prices are Friday’s close, before Monday’s US session.</p><p class="muted">Prices/vendor X: 2026-09-25 · Selected X windows: through 2026-09-27 · Local-mail collection attempted through 2026-09-28T13:01:36.2754640Z</p><div class="market"><h2 style="margin-top:0">Market mood · source dates matter</h2><p>Completed 2026-09-25 session: SPY +0.54%, QQQ +0.46% (Bloomberg price returns). CNN: last verified reading 35.1/100 (fear), timestamp 2026-09-23T12:49:16+00:00. PARTIAL LIVE-MOOD COVERAGE: today’s endpoint request was blocked; no new extreme entry is claimed.</p><p>Editorial watchlists combine reviewed evidence with quantitative context. They are not score rankings or measured fund holdings.</p></div><section class="market"><h2>Source availability this run</h2><p>Prices and vendor X through 25 September. Existing stock history reused; SPY/QQQ refreshed incrementally through 25 September with overlapping return-index units reconciled. No Monday closing return or causal event return is claimed.</p><p>CNN’s endpoint was blocked again. The last verified reading remains 35.1/100, Fear, dated 23 September. The updated ETF history can complete outcomes for old signals, but cannot establish a new CNN crossing today.</p><p>Local Outlook coverage stops at 24 September 16:51 UTC: 417 message headers screened and 141 bodies retrieved. Six selected original messages/publications were additionally read in authenticated Outlook web, including fresh Monday desk comments and the missing Damnang article. This is a partial review, not complete 25–28 September mailbox coverage. Linked reports, attachments and embedded charts were not fully extracted. The resume cursor remains conservative.</p><p>Selected-X rolling counts end 27 September and are compared with the last delivered window ending 23 September; those windows overlap. Monday posts are individually dated and excluded from complete-day totals. A selected feed is not all of X; author counts include possible affiliates. Missing vendor scores are not replaced with old scores.</p></section><section><h2>Strong / rising / crowded interest</h2><div class="grid"><article><div class="eyebrow">Emerging narrative: expert specificity ahead of broad attention</div><h3><a href="crowding-tracker.html#ticker=LITE">LITE</a></h3><p>Conor O’Mara relays Sherman’s shift toward a larger networking share of NVIDIA systems and explicitly favors Lumentum. Damnang separately argues that scarce component suppliers, especially lasers, can capture pricing power that manufacturers may not. This is useful thematic convergence; only the Jefferies note makes the direct LITE selection. Six selected posts across four authors still indicate a small audience.</p><div class="metrics">Close 2026-09-25: 1d +1.4%; 5d +1.2%. Score N/A/100 (vs last delivery N/A). Vendor X/5d 39 → 60; tone 0.006. Selected X/7d: 5 → 6 posts vs prior delivery / 4 authors; largest author 33%.</div><p><b>Change:</b> Recovered substantive optics research missed by the stale local mailbox; stronger explanation of who captures value, rather than a large jump in tweet count.</p><details><summary>What to watch · Medium; narrow social breadth</summary><p><b>1–5 sessions:</b> Watch whether independent analysts and users broaden the discussion beyond repeated desk summaries.</p><p><b>2–4 weeks:</b> Look for laser shipments, supply expansion and margin/estimate revisions; CPO versus NPO yields and timing remain unresolved.</p></details><p class="sources"><a href="#source-web26-conor">Jefferies specialist sales / Conor O’Mara; Asia trip with Sherman (2026-09-26)</a> · <a href="#source-web24-damnang">Damnang Research / Damnang and Aurelion Research (2026-09-24)</a></p></article><article><div class="eyebrow">New catalyst against previously cooling attention</div><h3><a href="crowding-tracker.html#ticker=NVDA">NVDA</a></h3><p>NVIDIA announced an additional $150bn buyback authorization, bringing the remaining authorization to $235bn, with expected execution through FY2028. Rand expects a near-term positive reaction but doubts a buyback alone resolves generalists’ AI-return concerns. Monday’s agent-safety announcement adds a product catalyst. Friday’s low score and falling vendor mentions precede both announcements.</p><div class="metrics">Close 2026-09-25: 1d +0.2%; 5d +1.3%. Score 22.6/100 (vs last delivery -17.2). Vendor X/5d 10,035 → 7,328; tone 0.010. Selected X/7d: 80 → 69 posts vs prior delivery / 29 authors; largest author 17%.</div><p><b>Change:</b> A verified capital-return announcement and new product arrive after a quieter quantitative attention window. Authorization is not completed repurchases or evidence of new fund buying.</p><details><summary>What to watch · Medium; announcement verified, persistence untested</summary><p><b>1–5 sessions:</b> Test whether discussion and price strength persist after the initial news, rather than treating repeated release headlines as independent conviction.</p><p><b>2–4 weeks:</b> Track actual capital return, adoption and generalist participation; watch the ROI debate rather than assuming a lasting re-rating.</p></details><p class="sources"><a href="#source-public:nvda-buyback">NVIDIA newsroom / NVIDIA (2026-09-28)</a> · <a href="#source-web28-barclays">Barclays TMT desk / Jeffrey Rand; relaying Tom O’Malley and Tim Long (2026-09-28)</a> · <a href="#source-x:2104527754523771387">Selected X post / firstadopter (2026-09-28)</a></p></article><article><div class="eyebrow">Broad enthusiasm / high attention; incremental buyers need testing</div><h3><a href="crowding-tracker.html#ticker=META">META</a></h3><p>Rothschild’s Falkiner relays Rowan Adley’s platform/ecosystem bull case after Connect. JPM’s Silver simultaneously relays reports of scaling friction, and Bernstein’s earlier desk reaction was underwhelming. Selected discussion broadened to 127 posts and 46 authors, while Friday fell after a strong week. The high score captures attention as well as tone, not investor positioning.</p><div class="metrics">Close 2026-09-25: 1d -3.3%; 5d +12.9%. Score 96.0/100 (vs last delivery +18.3). Vendor X/5d 373 → 626; tone 0.007. Selected X/7d: 89 → 127 posts vs prior delivery / 46 authors; largest author 14%.</div><p><b>Change:</b> The bullish platform narrative now has visible operational counterarguments; expansion in discussion is stronger evidence than an assumption that all new discussion is bullish.</p><details><summary>What to watch · Medium</summary><p><b>1–5 sessions:</b> Watch independent usage, reliability and Monday’s response; distinguish outside adoption from management promotion.</p><p><b>2–4 weeks:</b> Require evidence that usage converts into monetization without disproportionate compute cost; competitors may narrow the novelty advantage.</p></details><p class="sources"><a href="#source-web28-rothschild">Rothschild &amp; Co Redburn / Madeleine Falkiner; relaying Rowan Adley and house analysts (2026-09-28)</a> · <a href="#source-web28-jpm-silver">J.P. Morgan TMT specialist sales / Scott Silver; relaying Marcus Diebel and Doug Anmuth (2026-09-28)</a> · <a href="#source-4be34ee57271">Bernstein / Tyler Seidman (2026-09-24)</a></p></article><article><div class="eyebrow">Enterprise-agent adoption narrative gains broader sponsorship</div><h3><a href="crowding-tracker.html#ticker=MSFT">MSFT</a></h3><p>Dan Niles names Microsoft as a favored enterprise route into AI, consistent with the recent Copilot reactions. NVIDIA’s new safety-platform release also lists Microsoft in the ecosystem. These are investor opinion and product participation, not a new contract value. The positive Friday response accompanies modestly broader selected discussion, even as vendor publication volume eased.</p><div class="metrics">Close 2026-09-25: 1d +3.7%; 5d +4.5%. Score 71.3/100 (vs last delivery -2.9). Vendor X/5d 2,299 → 2,212; tone 0.012. Selected X/7d: 29 → 34 posts vs prior delivery / 18 authors; largest author 18%.</div><p><b>Change:</b> A weekend investor endorsement adds a different source to the product discussion; the underlying Copilot launch remains one event.</p><details><summary>What to watch · Medium</summary><p><b>1–5 sessions:</b> Watch customer usage reports and continued breadth beyond launch-day repetitions.</p><p><b>2–4 weeks:</b> Track paid agent usage, deployment and compute economics; product distribution needs to translate into incremental revenue.</p></details><p class="sources"><a href="#source-x:2104344675461054479">Selected X post / DanielTNiles (2026-09-27)</a> · <a href="#source-public:nvda-safety">NVIDIA newsroom / NVIDIA (2026-09-28)</a></p></article><article><div class="eyebrow">Neglected/improving-conviction candidate within negative aggregate tone</div><h3><a href="crowding-tracker.html#ticker=GOOG">GOOG</a></h3><p>Niles singles out Google as his preferred name among perceived agentic-disruption losers, pointing to an expected model response and Android distribution. That is an explicitly bullish opinion, not a verified new position. Vendor tone remains negative and selected-post count has fallen, so this is a divergence to monitor rather than an established sentiment turn.</p><div class="metrics">Close 2026-09-25: 1d +0.6%; 5d -1.0%. Score 54.4/100 (vs last delivery +11.9). Vendor X/5d 2,206 → 2,552; tone -0.041. Selected X/7d: 144 → 121 posts vs prior delivery / 36 authors; largest author 39%.</div><p><b>Change:</b> A new positive named-investor view challenges the disruption narrative; the model-launch expectation remains that author’s expectation.</p><details><summary>What to watch · Medium-low; one explicit new investor view</summary><p><b>1–5 sessions:</b> Look for independent product evaluations, broader positive discussion and a sustained turn in price.</p><p><b>2–4 weeks:</b> Test whether distribution and usage support economics; do not substitute a rumored launch for revenue evidence.</p></details><p class="sources"><a href="#source-x:2104344675461054479">Selected X post / DanielTNiles (2026-09-27)</a></p></article></div></section><section><h2>Weaker conviction / easing attention</h2><div class="grid"><article><div class="eyebrow">Softer institutional conviction despite positive social tone</div><h3><a href="crowding-tracker.html#ticker=MU">MU</a></h3><p>Rand reports more cautious memory sentiment, particularly in Asia, and says even a larger beat may not change the debate. Conor’s relay also questions how long strong memory pricing lasts. This contrasts with constructive Funda/Paradis discussion and a positive week in the stock. The issue is the incremental catalyst and expectations, not proof of deteriorating current demand.</p><div class="metrics">Close 2026-09-25: 1d +0.2%; 5d +6.5%. Score 70.2/100 (vs last delivery +1.2). Vendor X/5d 3,978 → 4,610; tone 0.073. Selected X/7d: 29 → 36 posts vs prior delivery / 15 authors; largest author 25%.</div><p><b>Change:</b> Fresh desk feedback weakens the inference that more positive posts imply more willing buyers ahead of earnings.</p><details><summary>What to watch · Medium</summary><p><b>1–5 sessions:</b> Watch the reaction to the print and guidance against investor expectations; a beat without follow-through would matter.</p><p><b>2–4 weeks:</b> Track pricing durability, supply and whether long-term agreements change conviction through a weaker cycle.</p></details><p class="sources"><a href="#source-web28-barclays">Barclays TMT desk / Jeffrey Rand; relaying Tom O’Malley and Tim Long (2026-09-28)</a> · <a href="#source-web26-conor">Jefferies specialist sales / Conor O’Mara; Asia trip with Sherman (2026-09-26)</a> · <a href="#source-x:2104402999997325484">Selected X post / FundaAI (2026-09-28)</a></p></article><article><div class="eyebrow">Execution-risk skepticism / controversy drives discussion</div><h3><a href="crowding-tracker.html#ticker=ORCL">ORCL</a></h3><p>Rothschild reiterates Sell and shifts emphasis toward site readiness and the financial strain of bringing capacity online, extending caution to NBIS and CRWV. This is one house framework, not three independent confirmations. Oracle’s previously relayed statement that Jupiter remains on schedule still stands; no new cancellation or completed delay was established today.</p><div class="metrics">Close 2026-09-25: 1d -1.7%; 5d -7.1%. Score 36.9/100 (vs last delivery +9.5). Vendor X/5d 3,223 → 2,417; tone -0.005. Selected X/7d: 7 → 20 posts vs prior delivery / 14 authors; largest author 15%.</div><p><b>Change:</b> A new house argument adds context to rising selected discussion and a weak price week. Greater attention here is not greater optimism.</p><details><summary>What to watch · Medium</summary><p><b>1–5 sessions:</b> Watch permits and verified construction milestones rather than repeated force-majeure headlines.</p><p><b>2–4 weeks:</b> Follow delivery, financing and acceptance under the existing Jupiter execution record; no new compute commitment is counted.</p></details><p class="sources"><a href="#source-web28-rothschild">Rothschild &amp; Co Redburn / Madeleine Falkiner; relaying Rowan Adley and house analysts (2026-09-28)</a> · <a href="#source-a0eca7b30e31">replytoanalyst@markit.esp.db.com / Brad Zelnick - Deutsche Bank (2026-09-24)</a></p></article><article><div class="eyebrow">Contested conviction after a strong rally</div><h3><a href="crowding-tracker.html#ticker=SHOP">SHOP</a></h3><p>Rothschild warns that Meta could internalize storefront and commerce functions. The earlier MS view argues that losing the Shop Pay button need not remove the underlying payments economics. Friday declined, but the week remained strongly positive. This is disagreement and possible vulnerability after enthusiasm, not consensus selling or proof of disintermediation.</p><div class="metrics">Close 2026-09-25: 1d -2.0%; 5d +10.7%. Score 88.4/100 (vs last delivery -0.4). Vendor X/5d 208 → 484; tone 0.017. Selected X/7d: 6 → 5 posts vs prior delivery / 4 authors; largest author 40%.</div><p><b>Change:</b> A fresh caution confronts a substantive bullish rebuttal. Few selected authors make the apparent consensus fragile.</p><details><summary>What to watch · Medium-low; conflicting business-model views</summary><p><b>1–5 sessions:</b> Watch whether independent commentary addresses the payments-versus-discovery distinction and whether the rally holds.</p><p><b>2–4 weeks:</b> Track merchant checkout control, processing economics and partnership implementation; require operating evidence to resolve the debate.</p></details><p class="sources"><a href="#source-web28-rothschild">Rothschild &amp; Co Redburn / Madeleine Falkiner; relaying Rowan Adley and house analysts (2026-09-28)</a> · <a href="#source-72b5ce85ffae">Morgan Stanley / Thomas Wigg (2026-09-24)</a></p></article><article><div class="eyebrow">Cooling attention; fresh product counterpoint</div><h3><a href="crowding-tracker.html#ticker=CRM">CRM</a></h3><p>Vendor mentions and the score contracted sharply while tone stayed mildly positive. NVIDIA’s release now identifies an OpenShell/Slack integration, a constructive counterpoint that has not yet appeared in Friday’s score. Cooling attention is not a bearish earnings call, and this product mention does not by itself establish a recovery.</p><div class="metrics">Close 2026-09-25: 1d -1.8%; 5d -1.6%. Score 18.3/100 (vs last delivery -25.7). Vendor X/5d 288 → 66; tone 0.019. Selected X/7d: 8 → 8 posts vs prior delivery / 6 authors; largest author 25%.</div><p><b>Change:</b> The lower attention baseline precedes Monday’s integration news; keep the event and the older quantitative window separate.</p><details><summary>What to watch · Low on crowding; quantitative cooling is observable</summary><p><b>1–5 sessions:</b> Watch whether the integration attracts substantive independent usage commentary beyond the announcement.</p><p><b>2–4 weeks:</b> Require paid adoption, retention or estimate changes before upgrading the signal from cooling attention to improving fundamentals.</p></details><p class="sources"><a href="#source-public:nvda-safety">NVIDIA newsroom / NVIDIA (2026-09-28)</a></p></article><article><div class="eyebrow">Cooling attention despite a live agent-security theme</div><h3><a href="crowding-tracker.html#ticker=PANW">PANW</a></h3><p>Vendor discussion cooled despite positive tone; the selected feed found no matched original posts in the completed seven-day window. That is feed coverage, not zero market discussion. NVIDIA lists PANW among safety-platform collaborators, while the earlier MS sales note favored cyber enablers. Participation does not yet quantify incremental bookings.</p><div class="metrics">Close 2026-09-25: 1d -3.9%; 5d +3.1%. Score 26.2/100 (vs last delivery -0.7). Vendor X/5d 545 → 282; tone 0.044. Selected X/7d: 0 → 0 posts vs prior delivery / 0 authors; largest author N/A%.</div><p><b>Change:</b> A new sector product event offers a test of whether attention resumes; Friday’s metrics cannot already reflect Monday’s release.</p><details><summary>What to watch · Medium-low</summary><p><b>1–5 sessions:</b> Watch customer and analyst responses that identify PANW’s specific paid role, rather than generic cyber enthusiasm.</p><p><b>2–4 weeks:</b> Look for attach rates and bookings conversion; consider whether open infrastructure complements or competes with paid products.</p></details><p class="sources"><a href="#source-public:nvda-safety">NVIDIA newsroom / NVIDIA (2026-09-28)</a> · <a href="#source-72b5ce85ffae">Morgan Stanley / Thomas Wigg (2026-09-24)</a></p></article></div></section><h2>Quality-source notes</h2><article><h3>Positioning: interested clients are not the same as fresh longs</h3><p>JPM’s Europe feedback describes investors inclined to buy AI hardware but few materially adding exposure. Barclays reports prior-day flow modestly better to sell overall, with long-only selling and HF buying. Paradis reports strong recent Mag7/semis buying from a different, unverified flow dataset. Different windows, venues and cohorts explain why these observations cannot be combined into one market-wide crowding statistic; neither broker identifies how much HF buying was short covering.</p><p class="sources"><a href="#source-web28-jpm-silver">J.P. Morgan TMT specialist sales / Scott Silver; relaying Marcus Diebel and Doug Anmuth (2026-09-28)</a> · <a href="#source-web28-barclays">Barclays TMT desk / Jeffrey Rand; relaying Tom O’Malley and Tim Long (2026-09-28)</a> · <a href="#source-x:2104494787927675318">Selected X post / ParadisLabs (2026-09-28)</a></p></article><article><h3>AMD: strong CPU thesis, but the expectations debate is no longer one-sided</h3><p>Barclays relays O’Malley’s larger CPU opportunity and preference for AMD. Conor’s Sherman relay is constructive on CPU growth but pushes back on the most aggressive Venice assumptions. The already-high score and strong week make this a crowded narrative to test, not a clean early discovery. CPU economics, GPU inference benchmarks and rack timing are separate claims.</p><p class="sources"><a href="#source-web28-barclays">Barclays TMT desk / Jeffrey Rand; relaying Tom O’Malley and Tim Long (2026-09-28)</a> · <a href="#source-web26-conor">Jefferies specialist sales / Conor O’Mara; Asia trip with Sherman (2026-09-26)</a></p></article><article><h3>Damnang: CIEN and FN are research candidates, not newly disclosed buys</h3><p>Damnang and Aurelion distinguish Ciena’s networking/pricing opportunity from Fabrinet’s manufacturing/yield economics. They are positive on the businesses but explicitly have not added either to the Aurelion Index, citing valuation and visibility. This substantive 24 September publication was recovered through web mail; it is separate from the administrative subscription message. No Verissimo forwarding chain was established for this item.</p><p class="sources"><a href="#source-web24-damnang">Damnang Research / Damnang and Aurelion Research (2026-09-24)</a></p></article><article><h3>SemiAnalysis: distinguish technical evidence from a stock endorsement</h3><p>The Panther Lake teardown documents shipped 18A silicon and explicitly limits the density-lead claim versus newer foundry nodes. That is higher-quality technical evidence than a generic bullish post, but not proof of foundry profitability, yields or a new long position. Daniel Grozdea’s forward and the publisher’s X promotion are one underlying publication, not independent confirmations. Selected introductory and concluding passages were reviewed; microscopy images were not independently analyzed.</p><p class="sources"><a href="#source-web26-semianalysis">SemiAnalysis / SemiAnalysis STEEL team / Dylan Patel; forwarded by Daniel Grozdea (2026-09-26)</a></p></article><h2>CNN extreme entries</h2><p>Retained &lt;=30 new-entry study: SPY mean +0.67% / up 74%, QQQ mean +1.14% / up 68%; N=38. Both mean intervals include zero. Latest completed close-to-close episode: 2026-09-15 → 2026-09-22, SPY +2.36%, QQQ +6.20%. These are historical associations, not current trade signals.</p><p><a href="cnn-next-week.html">Explore CNN timing and uncertainty</a></p><section id="score-return-email" style="background:#101c2e;color:#e4edfa;padding:23px;font:14px/1.6 Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif;border-radius:10px;margin-top:24px"><h2 style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:21px;color:#e4edfa">Score → next 1 and 2 weeks</h2><p>Prices through 2026-09-25. Historical conditional returns for the existing attention &amp; sentiment score (0–100), which blends attention and tone. Specialist-sales judgments are separate and are not retroactively scored. These means are reference analogs, not forecasts or price targets.</p><p>USD pooled reference: 75 historically eligible names. All-band means: 1w +0.9%; 2w +1.9%. N counts stock observations; shared market dates are not independent trials.</p><table cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse;margin:14px 0;width:100%;font-family:Segoe UI,Arial,sans-serif"><thead><tr><th style="background:#1b304b;color:#dce8f7;padding:9px 10px;border:1px solid #3a4d66;text-align:left;font-size:12px">Score band</th><th style="background:#1b304b;color:#dce8f7;padding:9px 10px;border:1px solid #3a4d66;text-align:left;font-size:12px">1w mean</th><th style="background:#1b304b;color:#dce8f7;padding:9px 10px;border:1px solid #3a4d66;text-align:left;font-size:12px">1w median</th><th style="background:#1b304b;color:#dce8f7;padding:9px 10px;border:1px solid #3a4d66;text-align:left;font-size:12px">1w up / N</th><th style="background:#1b304b;color:#dce8f7;padding:9px 10px;border:1px solid #3a4d66;text-align:left;font-size:12px">2w mean</th><th style="background:#1b304b;color:#dce8f7;padding:9px 10px;border:1px solid #3a4d66;text-align:left;font-size:12px">2w median</th><th style="background:#1b304b;color:#dce8f7;padding:9px 10px;border:1px solid #3a4d66;text-align:left;font-size:12px">2w up / N</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding